---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started' }];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <!-- Hero header -->
  <header class="docHero">
    <p class="docEyebrow">Quick Start Guide</p>
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="docLead">
      OpenFlows orchestrates autonomous AI agents that discover issues, write code, review it, 
      and merge pull requests without human intervention. Give it a GitHub repo and some issues, 
      and OpenFlows coordinates a team of AI agents that plan the work, write the code, review it 
      adversarially, and ship reviewed PRs — without you writing a single line of code.
    </p>
    <div class="docMeta">
      <span class="docMetaItem">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
        ~10 min read
      </span>
      <span class="docMetaSep"></span>
      <span class="docMetaItem">4 steps</span>
      <span class="docMetaSep"></span>
      <span class="docMetaItem">Beginner</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">⚠️ Content Update in Progress</p>
        <div class="calloutText">This page is being updated to reflect the current OpenFlows workflow. Some information may be temporarily outdated.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Why architecture-first</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          AI can generate code against a spec, but it can't write the spec. As models make boilerplate cheap, 
          the real difficulty shifts <em>up the stack</em> — into architectural thinking, product judgment, 
          and security awareness. OpenFlows encodes that discipline: a declared flow graph (PocketFlow), 
          typed SharedStore state contracts, an explicit routing table, and recovery built into every step. 
          <strong>Engineering goes in, software comes out.</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 1: Prerequisites -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="prerequisites">
    <span class="stepNumber">1</span>
    Prerequisites
  </h2>
  <div class="prereqGrid">
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>Docker 24+</h3>
      <p>Required for running the Docker Compose stack with Coder, Redis, and OpenFlows.</p>
      <a href="https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get Docker</a>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>Git 2.x+</h3>
      <p>Required for cloning repositories and version control operations.</p>
      <a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get Git</a>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>GitHub OAuth App</h3>
      <p>Required for Coder external authentication. Create a GitHub OAuth App for your organization.</p>
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/creating-an-oauth-app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Create OAuth App</a>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>LLM Provider</h3>
      <p>At least one LLM provider configured in the Coder dashboard (AI Settings → Coder Agents → Models).</p>
      <p>Configure in Coder dashboard</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Coder governs where agents run — OpenFlows governs how they coordinate</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          The integration is deliberate and asymmetrical:
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Coder</strong> provides the governed environment: ephemeral workspaces, control-plane AI agents, model governance, identity, audit logging, cost tracking. The workspace has zero AI software and zero LLM keys.</li>
            <li><strong>OpenFlows</strong> provides the brain: the flow graph, typed SharedStore contracts, the Node trait's <code>prep → exec → post</code> separation, and the FORGE↔SENTINEL planning cycle.</li>
          </ul>
          Coder Agents run in the <strong>control plane</strong> (not in workspaces). They execute tool calls by connecting to workspaces over the same secure tunnel as IDEs.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="install">
    <span class="stepNumber">2</span>
    Quick Start Installation
  </h2>
  <p>The recommended setup uses Docker Compose to bring up the entire OpenFlows stack including Coder, Redis, and the orchestration engine.</p>

  <div class="install-code-wrapper">
    <div class="codeBlock install-code">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
        <button class="copyBtn">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2" ry="2"></rect>
            <path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"></path>
          </svg>
          Copy
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/openflows.git
cd openflows
cp .env.example .env   # edit .env with your GitHub OAuth app credentials

# 1. Start the infrastructure stack
docker compose up -d

# 2. Bootstrap OpenFlows (creates admin, pushes templates, verifies config)
cargo run -p openflows --bin openflows -- bootstrap

# 3. Add a tenant (links GitHub via Coder external auth, creates nexus workspace)
cargo run -p openflows --bin openflows -- tenant add owner/repo --name my-team

# 4. Create a GitHub issue in your repo — OpenFlows picks it up automatically</code></pre>
    </div>
    
    <div class="step-breakdown">
      <div class="step-marker">
        <span class="step-number">1</span>
        <span class="step-label">Clone & Setup</span>
      </div>
      <div class="step-marker">
        <span class="step-number">2</span>
        <span class="step-label">Bootstrap</span>
      </div>
      <div class="step-marker">
        <span class="step-number">3</span>
        <span class="step-label">Add Tenant</span>
      </div>
      <div class="step-marker">
        <span class="step-number">4</span>
        <span class="step-label">Create Issue</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">The openflows-harness CLI</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          Each worker workspace gets a small <code>openflows-harness</code> binary. The Coder Agent invokes it via shell (guided by skills) to read/write the Redis SharedStore with typed, validated schemas. Agents never run <code>redis-cli</code> directly — the harness is the only Redis client in a workspace.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>See the <a href="/docs/getting-started/installation">Installation Guide</a> for detailed configuration and alternative setups.</p>

  <!-- Step 3: Multi-Tenancy -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="configure">
    <span class="stepNumber">3</span>
    Multi-Tenancy Model
  </h2>
  <p>
    One Coder server serves many teams. Each tenant = a real Coder user + a repo binding + an <code>openflows-nexus</code> workspace. 
    Tenants are isolated by Coder RBAC and per-tenant Redis keyspace prefixes (<code>ns:&#123;tenant&#125;:...</code>).
  </p>

  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
      <button class="copyBtn">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          <rect x="9" y="9" width="13" height="13" rx="2" ry="2"></rect>
          <path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"></path>
        </svg>
        Copy
      </button>
    </div>
    <pre><code># Add a new tenant
cargo run -p openflows --bin openflows -- tenant add another-org/another-repo --name team-b</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Environment Configuration</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          Copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and configure:
          <ul>
            <li>GitHub OAuth App credentials for Coder external auth</li>
            <li>Coder deployment settings</li>
            <li>Repository configurations</li>
            <li>LLM provider settings (configured in Coder dashboard)</li>
          </ul>
          The bootstrap process handles template deployment and initial setup.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 3.1: Plug-and-Play Extension -->
  <h3 id="extension">3.1 Plug-and-Play Extension</h3>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is designed for easy extension without code changes:
  </p>

  <div class="extension-grid">
    <div class="extension-item">
      <h4>Add a skill</h4>
      <p>Drop a directory in <code>orchestration/plugin/skills/</code> with a <code>SKILL.md</code>, list it in <code>registry.json</code> under the role's <code>skills</code> array. No code change.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="extension-item">
      <h4>Add an MCP server</h4>
      <p>Add it to the role's <code>mcp</code> object in <code>registry.json</code>, or register it centrally in the Coder dashboard (AI Settings → MCP Servers). Both coexist.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="extension-item">
      <h4>Enable a new model</h4>
      <p>Configure it in the Coder dashboard (AI Settings → Coder Agents → Models). Reference it in <code>registry.json</code> via the <code>model</code> field.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="extension-item">
      <h4>Development updates</h4>
      <p>After code changes, run <code>./update-binaries.sh</code> to rebuild and update all workspaces with fresh binaries.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">How It Works</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          OpenFlows runs a team of AI agents that collaborate just like a real engineering team:
          <pre style="margin: 0; padding: 1rem; background: var(--color-parchment-canvas); border-radius: var(--radius-md); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.875rem;">
You create a GitHub issue → NEXUS picks it up → FORGE writes code → SENTINEL reviews adversarially
→ VESSEL merges green PRs → LORE documents → you get a merged PR</pre>
          You stay in the loop only when needed — security concerns, ambiguous specs, or major decisions.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 4: Verify -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="verify">
    <span class="stepNumber">4</span>
    Create Your First Issue
  </h2>
  <p>Once OpenFlows is running with your tenant configured, create a GitHub issue in your repository. OpenFlows will automatically:</p>
  
  <ol>
    <li><strong>NEXUS detects</strong> the new issue on its next poll cycle</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE is assigned</strong> the ticket with a unique ID</li>
    <li><strong>Ephemeral Coder workspace</strong> is provisioned for the FORGE worker</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE writes code</strong> against the agreed contract</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL reviews</strong> every segment adversarially</li>
    <li><strong>VESSEL handles</strong> CI, conflicts, and merge</li>
    <li><strong>LORE documents</strong> the changes automatically</li>
    <li><strong>Workspace is torn down</strong> after merge — no lingering state</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">The Team</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Agent</th>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Role</th>
                <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">What it does</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);"><strong>NEXUS</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Orchestrator</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Assigns issues, coordinates the team, owns recovery, notifies you when needed</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);"><strong>FORGE</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Builder</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Writes code against an agreed contract, creates branches, opens PRs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);"><strong>SENTINEL</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Reviewer</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Adversarially reviews code for security, quality, and test coverage</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);"><strong>VESSEL</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">DevOps</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Monitors CI, handles merge conflicts, squash-merges green PRs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);"><strong>LORE</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Writer</td>
                <td style="padding: 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);">Documents decisions, updates changelogs, maintains project history</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 5: Next Steps -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="next-steps">
    <span class="stepNumber">5</span>
    Next Steps
  </h2>
  
  <div class="docCards">
    <a href="/docs/architecture/agent-roles" class="docCard">
      <div class="docCardIcon">🧭</div>
      <div class="docCardTitle">Agent Roles</div>
      <div class="docCardDesc">Understand each agent's contract, recovery behavior, and workspace lifecycle.</div>
    </a>
    
    <a href="/docs/faq" class="docCard">
      <div class="docCardIcon">❓</div>
      <div class="docCardTitle">FAQ & Troubleshooting</div>
      <div class="docCardDesc">Coder connectivity, tenant setup, token scopes, and common fixes.</div>
    </a>
    
    <a href="https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/openflows" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="docCard">
      <div class="docCardIcon">🐙</div>
      <div class="docCardTitle">GitHub Repository</div>
      <div class="docCardDesc">Source code, issues, and discussions for OpenFlows development.</div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Documentation</p>
        <div class="calloutText">
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/docs/architecture/system-design">System Design</a> - PocketFlow engine and SharedStore architecture</li>
            <li><a href="/docs/coder-compatibility">Coder Compatibility</a> - Version compatibility and verification</li>
            <li><a href="/docs/tenancy">Tenancy</a> - Multi-tenant model and Redis namespacing</li>
            <li><a href="/docs/governance">Governance</a> - AI governance controls and network policy</li>
            <li><a href="/docs/extending">Extending</a> - Adding skills, MCP servers, and models</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>



  <div class="feedbackFooter">
    <p>Was this guide helpful? <a href="https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/openflows/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discuss on GitHub ↗</a></p>
  </div>
  
  <style>
    .extension-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin: 2rem 0;
    }
    
    .extension-item {
      background: var(--color-bone);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: 1.5rem;
      transition: border-color var(--transition-fast), box-shadow var(--transition-fast);
    }
    
    .extension-item:hover {
      border-color: var(--color-fog);
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
    }
    
    .extension-item h4 {
      margin-top: 0;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      font-size: var(--font-size-base);
      font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
      color: var(--color-ink);
    }
    
    .extension-item p {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
      color: var(--color-graphite);
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
    
    /* Update for config tabs removal */
    .tabTabs, .tabContent {
      display: none;
    }
    
    .stepHeading[data-step="3"] {
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }
  </style>
  
  <script>
    // Remove deprecated tab functionality since we simplified the page
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
      // Hide any remaining tab elements
      const tabElements = document.querySelectorAll('.tabTabs, .tabContent, .tabTab');
      tabElements.forEach(el => {
        if (el.parentElement) {
          el.parentElement.removeChild(el);
        }
      });
      
      // Update step numbers if needed
      const steps = document.querySelectorAll('.stepNumber');
      if (steps.length > 0) {
        // Already updated to 5 steps
      }
    });
  </script>
</DocsLayout>
